# Git normalises line endings on commit and checks out whatever the platform
# prefers, which on Windows means everything comes back CRLF. That stays
# invisible until a text file crosses into Linux — and this repo's files do,
# every time the Docker image is built:
#
#   * a shell script with CRLF fails inside a container with "no such file or
#     directory" pointing at a file that plainly exists, because the kernel
#     reads the \r as part of the interpreter path on the shebang line;
#   * a CSV parser expecting LF picks up a trailing \r on every last field;
#   * and every checkout rewrites whole files, so `git diff` fills with changes
#     that are not changes — see labels.csv, 25 lines "modified" into
#     themselves.
#
# eol=lf pins the working copy to LF on every platform. Every editor worth
# using handles it; only very old Notepad does not.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Never transform these. Git's own detection is good, but a corrupted model
# checkpoint or PNG is a bad way to find the edge of it.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.keras binary
*.pdf   binary
*.docx  binary
*.zip   binary
*.ico   binary
